// Yoopanese (U.P. dialect) translation of Snow War tiers 145–288 (array indices 144–287).
window.YOOPER = window.YOOPER || {};
window.YOOPER.SNOW_WAR_TIERS = Object.assign(window.YOOPER.SNOW_WAR_TIERS || {}, {
  "Depots": { label: "Da Depots", desc: "Dey set up forward stores behind every sector, six of 'em holdin' fifteen hundred rounds apiece. Time from askin' to gettin' it at da line drops from twenty-five minutes to four. Two depots sit inside trebuchet range of da other side's works, an' dey get moved after da first one gets hit, at a loss of eleven hundred rounds." },
  "Self-Propelled Spreaders": { label: "Spreaders Dat Push Demselves", desc: "Dey mount da machines on sled frames an' tow 'em with teams, so da drive comes from da tow instead of da operator's legs. Da screw still turns only when da wheels turn, so da old safety behaviour stays just like it was, at ten times da output. A towed unit lays a hundred-and-forty-centimetre swath at a runnin' pace an' takes four people to stop, ya know." },
  "Mobile Batteries": { label: "Da Batteries on Runners", desc: "Dey put da engines on sled runners an' move 'em every night, four to a battery, so no firin' spot gets used twice. Movin' takes twenty minutes an' cuts counter-battery losses by three quarters. Da catch is accuracy: da first shot from a new spot lands sixty feet wide on average, eh." },
  "Forward Works": { label: "Da Forward Works", desc: "Everything out front of da bailey gets rebuilt to da new scale: four outworks a side at sixteen feet long, four feet high an' twenty inches thick, pushed sixty feet out, an' a redoubt fourteen feet across, six and a half feet high an' forty inches thick holdin' eight hundred rounds. Da fieldworks get recut right along with 'em, nine hundred feet of ready position against da old three hundred, worked by thirty-one people over three days, with da sling lanes widened to take engines. Defended depth from da outermost stake to da bailey wall comes out to two hundred and forty feet, which is just inside da range of da heaviest engine dat can reach it." },
  "Snow Citadel": { label: "Da Snow Citadel", desc: "A fortress with a bunch of chambers an' a roof dat actually holds: five rooms across a footprint averagin' thirty-four feet by twenty-two, forty feet on da long way, six feet of headroom in da middle, walls three feet thick. It gets a name, marked right at da door, an' every position on da map gets figured from it. Buildin' it took sixty person-hours. Dey don't give up da inner bailey, dey make it bigger: a hundred and sixty feet of circuit at seven feet high an' thirty-six inches thick, wrappin' sixteen hundred square feet of citadel an' ammo store, for another ninety person-hours. Garrison runs twenty-eight at da complex out of eighty-eight in da whole war." },
  "Citadel Blanketing": { label: "Tuckin' In Da Citadel", desc: "Da whole citadel gets kept under a coat: eleven crews on rotation, every bare surface redone within seventy-two hours of somebody walkin' on it. Dat eats nine tons of moved-around snow a day, none of it new. From da outer works da citadel looks like it's always, somehow, just been snowed on, an' everybody inside knows dat's a political statement, yah." },
  "Conscription": { label: "Da Draft", desc: "Showin' up ain't voluntary anymore. Every house across four blocks gets assessed at one body per two kids, which makes a roll of a hundred and forty. Ya get out of it for an injury, for havin' a workin' woodstove, or if you're one of da eleven crews already servin' engines. About seventy percent actually show." },
  "Heavy Trebuchets": { label: "Da Big Ol' Trebuchets", desc: "Counterweights go up to four hundred pounds of packed snow on frames dat need a crew of nine. Range gets out to four hundred feet an' one shot takes a foot of glazed wall right off da face. Dey build two, an' each one ties up its crew a whole day every time it gets moved." },
  "Standing Army": { label: "Da Standin' Army", desc: "It quits bein' casual once da roster hits ninety across both sides. Dere's four ranks, uniforms made outta scarves, an' a chain of command dat runs every order through one fella. Attendance gets checked every day, an' turnout stays over eighty percent eleven days straight." },
  "Spreader Conscription": { label: "Drafted onto Da Spreaders", desc: "Runnin' a machine stops bein' somethin' ya volunteer for an' starts bein' assigned, four hundred crews called up in a season. Da guild says an untrained guy lays an uneven bed, gets overruled, an' is proven right inside a month when average coverage variation goes from fourteen percent to thirty-one. Trainin' comes back, from da same folks, an' nobody says a word about it." },
  "Officer Corps": { label: "Da Officers", desc: "Givin' orders gets split off from fightin': eleven officers across both sides, an' not one of 'em has thrown a round in nine days. Orders get written down instead of hollered, about thirty a day, a third of 'em battery instructions. Time from decidin' to doin' at da front runs six minutes." },
  "Spreader Clauses": { label: "Da Spreader Clauses", desc: "Da settlements dey work out start havin' terms about machines: how many each side can keep, what ground can get resurfaced, an' a standin' rule against layin' cover where da other side can see it from its works. Dem clauses are more detailed dan da ones about throwin'. Both sides stick to da count, an' neither one sticks to da sight line, eh." },
  "Treaties": { label: "Da Treaties", desc: "Delegations of six draft an' sign formal agreements on flattened cardboard. Dey set boundaries to da foot, ammo limits at three hundred a side, a cap of four engines apiece, an' terms for when da fightin' starts back up. Four of 'em are in force at once, an' two of 'em disagree about da same line." },
  "Indirect Fire": { label: "Shootin' Blind", desc: "Engines get aimed at targets da crews can't even see, worked off corrections a spotter hollers down from da tower. Spottin' to correction takes ninety seconds, an' da hit rate on somethin' ya can't see settles around one in seven. Da back slope of every position turns into a target for da first time." },
  "Border Commission": { label: "Da Border Commission", desc: "A standin' bunch of nine gets seated to figure out what da treaties mean, even from both sides plus one neutral. Dey rule on forty-one disputes in a month, an' folks accept dem rulings in thirty-four. Boundary markers go in every fifty feet across two miles." },
  "Weather Falsification": { label: "Fakin' Da Weather", desc: "Dey use spreadin' big-time to fake a snowfall dat never happened, across nine square miles, to get school called off in a district dat got nuttin'. It works. Da record for dat day reads as a four-inch storm in every local account an' a dry day on every instrument, an' da mismatch still gets cited a thousand years later by both sides for opposite reasons, holy wah." },
  "Total Snow War": { label: "All-Out Snow War", desc: "A campaign across da whole region, seven neighborhoods, with offensives all lined up on one timetable. Da war's gone on nine days longer dan da storm dat started it, with better dan three hundred people in it. Daily use across every front goes past twelve thousand rounds, a third of it thrown by battery — forty engines workin', trebuchets an' torsion onagers in about equal numbers." },
  "Pattern Engines": { label: "Engines Off Da Same Pattern", desc: "Engine buildin' gets standardized to one drawin' — one frame, one arm length, one cup — so parts off any wreck fit any engine still standin'. Dey build ninety to da pattern in a month, against forty of mixed design still in service. Time to get a busted engine back on da line drops from two days to four hours." },
  "General Mobilization": { label: "Everybody Called Up", desc: "Every eligible body in da region gets called up at once — eleven hundred of 'em inside forty-eight hours. Production goes all to ammo, four thousand rounds a day. Attendance across four school districts drops under half, an' nobody writes it down dat week." },
  "Spreader Divisions": { label: "Da Spreader Divisions", desc: "Redistribution gets its own command, four divisions of nine hundred machines each, sittin' right alongside da batteries instead of under da supply train. Folks fight it for two seasons, an' one number settles it: fronts with a division attached hold three times as long as fronts without. From here on, ya salute a spreader officer." },
  "Blitz Doctrine": { label: "Da Blitz", desc: "Piles of ammo an' speed push fortification right out. Forts get walked away from mid-campaign for fast columns of eleven, each one carryin' four hundred rounds an' a light launcher on a sled, coverin' a quarter mile in under six minutes. Average fight length drops from fifty minutes to nine, dey punch six hundred feet past da line, an' da front moves up a block a day." },
  "Blitz Spreading": { label: "Blitz Spreadin'", desc: "Da doctrine flips over. Instead of resurfacin' ground ya already hold, columns of machines run out ahead of da advance layin' supply on ground nobody's taken yet, so da attack shows up on a bed it can pack from. Fronts move eleven miles in a week for da first time in da war. Better dan half da forward machines get lost, an' dey keep da doctrine anyway." },
  "Deep Raiding": { label: "Raidin' Way Behind Da Line", desc: "Parties of four carryin' sixty rounds apiece work up to two miles behind da other side's line for as long as three days, movin' nine miles a night. Dey go after depots, quarries, an' engine parks, not positions; nine get wrecked in a month, costin' maybe sixty thousand rounds. Only two raids in five reach a target at all, an' about a third of da parties don't make it back on schedule." },
  "Precision Batteries": { label: "Batteries Dat Hit Right On Da Nose", desc: "Firin' gets figured instead of eyeballed: range, arm length, counterweight an' snow density all worked out on paper before da first shot. Four engines shootin' one solution land inside a nine-foot circle at three hundred feet. Dey appoint a battery clerk, an' every rangin' book gets rolled into one table." },
  "Fortress Cities": { label: "Da Fortress Cities", desc: "Strongholds big enough to sleep deir own garrisons, forty to sixty folks apiece in forty-one rooms across a hundred and twenty feet by ninety, eight feet of headroom, behind four-foot walls. Tunnels inside hook up chambers dat hook up to more chambers still bein' dug, past nine hundred feet of mapped passage so far an' who knows how much more. Da bailey grows with 'em: five hundred and forty feet of circuit at nine feet high an' forty-eight inches thick, wrappin' eighteen thousand square feet of stronghold, store an' yard, for five hundred person-hours. Da trench an' tunnel network gets recut too, eleven hundred feet of trench four feet deep an' eight hundred feet of tunnel at nine, on nineteen entrances, seven of 'em hidden." },
  "City-Scale Spreaders": { label: "Spreaders Big as a Town", desc: "Dey build machines at town scale — troughs four metres across, screws run off wagon wheels — an' lay 'em across whole districts every night on a schedule. One unit covers eleven blocks in a shift. Folks stop tellin' da difference between weather an' maintenance, an' in three provincial dialects da word for snowfall turns into da word for da machine." },
  "Staff College": { label: "Da Staff College", desc: "Plannin' turns into somethin' dey teach. Twenty-two candidates a year study supply, frontage, an' da nine campaigns fought so far, in a course dat runs four months. Graduates plan three weeks out instead of one afternoon, an' campaigns run about twice as long." },
  "War Academy": { label: "Da War Academy", desc: "Newcomers get taught technique proper over six sessions. Dere's a right way to pack — four squeezes, three seconds — a right way to throw, a four-person drill for layin' an' loadin' an engine, an' a written-down order for pullin' back under fire. About forty students a class, an' eighty percent graduate." },
  "The Spreader Academy": { label: "Da Spreader Academy", desc: "Dey start a school dat teaches nuttin' but redistribution: gate tables, readin' da verge, buffer behaviour, an' four hundred hours of pushin'. Da entrance exam is one unbroken hundred-foot run, judged by eye. Da course opens with da graph-paper sketch, copied off a copy of a copy, one wheel still drawn bigger dan da other." },
  "Provinces": { label: "Da Provinces", desc: "Da territory gets split into eleven administrative units, each one with its own quarry, depot, engine park, an' roll of about two hundred. Orders from da center get to da farthest one in a day and a half. Three provinces make more dan dey use an' get taxed a fifth of output, about nine hundred rounds a week apiece." },
  "Continental Spreader Works": { label: "Da Spreader Works, Coast to Coast", desc: "Da empire turns redistribution into industry. Forty spreader works across da provinces crank out roller after roller off one pattern, moulded in cold-tough copolymer so da screws don't shatter at depth, an' keep a standin' park of eleven hundred machines. Whole fronts get resurfaced overnight, an' da phrase “out of ammunition” just kinda drops outta da language. Da works get guarded heavier dan da batteries dey supply." },
  "Imperial Gauge": { label: "Da Imperial Gauge", desc: "One size gets decreed for every work in da empire, an' dey set it on purpose above da biggest one already standin', so no fort meets it an' every fort's gotta grow. Glacis: five miles of cleared apron per settlement, a hundred and ten miles across da twenty-two, laid at five millimetres. Outworks: four to a side, twenty-four feet long, six feet high, thirty inches thick, pushed ninety feet forward. Trench: six feet deep an' four feet across da floor. Tunnel: fourteen feet down an' four feet wide, on thirty-one entrances, eleven of 'em hidden. Redoubt: twenty-two feet across, nine feet high, five feet thick, two thousand rounds in reserve. Main wall: five hundred and fifty feet long, fourteen feet high an' eight feet thick, firin' notches fourteen inches at da throat, six to a section, an' a tower eight feet above da parapet carryin' da sightline out to two hundred and twenty feet. Bailey: nine hundred feet of circuit at fourteen feet high an' six feet thick, wrappin' forty thousand square feet. Keep: two hundred feet by a hundred and fifty, twelve feet of headroom behind six-foot walls, ninety rooms. Surveyors go out ahead of da diggers with a marked pole an' a bound book an' report dat not one work in da empire meets da new gauge, which is da whole point. Da standard gets set above da biggest work instead of at it, on da stated reasonin' dat a standard ya already meet changes nuttin', an' on da unstated one dat da province dat wrote da decree can afford to build to it first." },
  "The Great Recut": { label: "Da Great Recut", desc: "Every fort in da empire gets blown up to da imperial gauge in one season — twenty-two settlements, somethin' like eighteen miles of trench an' nine of tunnel dug wider an' deeper, every wall raised an' thickened, every keep stretched out, by sixty thousand hands workin' off da same book. Each settlement's main wall gets laid out to da gauge at five hundred and fifty feet of unbroken face, fourteen feet high an' eight thick — two and a half times da length of da wall a whole two-fort alliance used to share, an' built for one settlement alone. Behind it each settlement's left with three quarters of a mile of trench an' three eighths of a mile of tunnel, twenty-one settlements makin' up fifteen and three quarter miles of da one an' seven and seven eighths of da other; da capital digs da rest. A round packed in one province now sits in any notch in any other, an engine built in da east rolls right into an emplacement cut in da west, an' a crew sent anywhere in da empire finds da trench floor right where deir feet expect it in da dark. Two forts won't do da enlargement an' keep deir old dimensions; both get abandoned inside da year, not by any order but 'cause nuttin' else in da empire fits 'em, an' da word for a thing built to no standard turns into da word for dem two forts." },
  "Snow Empire": { label: "Da Snow Empire", desc: "Fortifications ya can see from an upstairs window an' from da air, runnin' two and a half miles of continuous works at fourteen feet high an' eight feet thick. Da capital, which wrote da gauge an' built to it first, didn't quit dere: its main wall runs sixteen hundred and fifty feet, twenty-one feet high an' twelve thick, with firin' notches eighteen inches at da throat, eight to a section, an' a tower twelve feet above da parapet carryin' da sightline out to three hundred and thirty feet. Its bailey runs eighteen hundred feet of circuit at twenty-one feet high an' nine thick, wrappin' a hundred and sixty thousand square feet; its keep is three hundred feet by two hundred and twenty-five, eighteen feet of headroom behind nine-foot walls, two hundred rooms. Its redoubt is thirty-three feet across, thirteen and a half high an' seven and a half thick on three thousand rounds of reserve; its six outworks a side run thirty-six feet long, nine high an' forty-five inches thick, pushed a hundred and thirty-five feet forward; its trench is nine feet deep an' six across da floor an' runs two and a quarter miles, its tunnels twenty-one feet down an' six wide for a mile and an eighth on forty-seven entrances, seventeen hidden — three times da network of any other settlement — an' its glacis runs seven and a half miles. Twenty-one settlement walls of five hundred and fifty feet plus da capital's one make up da two and a half miles exactly. Outlyin' forts pay tribute at two thousand packed rounds a week an' get garrison support back. Twenty-two settlements are signed up, two are behind on payin' an' keep deir garrison support anyway, an' four more are still talkin'." },
  "The Spreader Ministry": { label: "Da Spreader Ministry", desc: "Redistribution turns into a department of state with a bigger budget dan da batteries', four thousand staff, an' say-so over what ground in da empire gets covered an' when. It answers to nobody who's ever pushed a machine. Its first published act is a list of resurfacin' priorities dat ranks fortifications below public roads, which sets off a constitutional crisis an' stands anyhow." },
  "Court Politics": { label: "Politics at Court", desc: "Decisions move inside. Nine advisors crowd around da commander, none of 'em holdin' a spot on da line, an' four of da last six top appointments went to folks who never built a wall. Two field commands change hands in a month for reasons dat never make it into da record." },
  "Hereditary Aristocracy": { label: "Da Hereditary Aristocracy", desc: "Command starts passin' down to younger brothers an' sisters by succession instead of merit, across eleven separate lines. Who's in charge gets fixed by birth order an' holds about four days before da first fella contests it. Average tenure of a hereditary commander runs thirty-one hours before somebody replaces 'em." },
  "Client Kingdoms": { label: "Da Client Kingdoms", desc: "Nine smaller outfits keep deir own flags, calendars, an' inside law while handin' over foreign policy completely. Each one sends three hundred bodies when asked an' gets protection back, a fixed ammo allowance of four thousand rounds a season, an' six engines it ain't allowed to export. Two try to renegotiate in da same season, an' one pulls it off." },
  "The Spreader Cartels": { label: "Da Spreader Cartels", desc: "Makin' da machines boils down to four houses holdin' da moulds between 'em, settin' price, pattern, an' delivery for every machine in da alliance. A screw costs eleven times what it did under da foundries an' breaks just as often. Two client kingdoms try moulding deir own, get cut off from spares, an' cave inside a season." },
  "Continental War": { label: "War Across Da Whole Continent", desc: "Separate towns' snow wars run into each other an' merge into one fight spannin' four hundred miles. Nobody in it can name every side — da count's at nineteen an' still goin' up — an' some get counted on two sides at once. All told, dey figure about eleven thousand are in it." },
  "Artillery Doctrine": { label: "Da Artillery Doctrine", desc: "Written doctrine covers da engines demselves — when to bunch 'em up, when to hold 'em back, an' how much of production dey get to eat. It sets da ratio at one engine per forty participants an' one third of all rounds. Three of da nineteen sides say no thanks an' keep doin' it deir own way." },
  "Doctrinal Councils": { label: "Da Doctrine Councils", desc: "Assemblies get called to settle questions of practice instead of territory: how dense a round can be, whether ice cores are okay, an' how old ya gotta be to play. Four councils sit in two years, one of 'em for eleven weeks, an' none of da four rulings gets accepted everywhere." },
  "The Great Schism": { label: "Da Great Schism", desc: "Da war splits on doctrine over whether ice-cored rounds are allowed, a question dat only came up once engines started throwin' 'em far enough to matter. Each side bans da other's practice an' enforces it at home with inspections at every checkpoint. Da split runs through thirty-one settlements an' cuts fourteen of 'em right down da middle." },
  "The Spreader Schism": { label: "Da Spreader Schism", desc: "Da split runs right through da machine. One bunch says laid snow is snow an' ya can fight over it like any other; da other says only fallen snow is legit an' everything laid is a forgery. Ya can't settle neither one with a tape measure, which is why it lasts. Both sides keep spreadin', an' both say da other side's coverage don't count." },
  "Settlement Law": { label: "Da Settlement Law", desc: "Written law takes over from worked-out custom: four hundred and twelve articles coverin' buildin' rights, quarry access, an' where ya stand if ya never threw. Courts sit in nine settlements an' hear about two hundred cases a year, a fifth of 'em about property instead of fightin'." },
  "Snow Civilization": { label: "Da Snow Civilization", desc: "Permanent buildings, permanent institutions, an' more dan four thousand folks livin' dere. Fightin' takes up under a fifth of da settlement's work now; da rest goes to buildin', food, mappin', an' upkeep. Ninety percent of da buildings still standin' are older dan da current campaign." },
  "Spreader Liturgy": { label: "Da Spreader Liturgy", desc: "Da first push of da season turns into a formal thing: da gate gets opened to da twelve-millimetre detent, a set three metres gets laid, an' da clutch check gets said out loud in words nobody can translate anymore. Da phrase in da middle of it is an eight-hundred-year corruption of “nothing snapped.” Both sides of da schism do it exactly da same, ya know." },
  "Oral Canon": { label: "Da Oral Canon", desc: "A fixed body of recited stuff takes shape — about two hundred pieces, none written down, kept by about forty specialists. Sayin' da whole canon takes eleven hours, an' dey do it twice a year. Put two reciters from far apart side by side an' dey agree on ninety-four percent of da lines." },
  "Founding Myths": { label: "Da Founding Myths", desc: "Folks tell origin stories about da first round ever thrown, in at least eight competin' versions. Dey contradict each other on every point dat matters — who threw it, how far, what year — an' ya can't check a one of 'em against a record. Six of da eight have never been written down." },
  "The Spreader Dynasty": { label: "Da Spreader Dynasty", desc: "Of da four cartel houses, one outlasts da rest: da family come down from da kid who drew da sketch, hangin' onto da original pattern an' supplyin' seven machines in ten across da alliance. Every unit it casts comes off dat same drawin', da eleven-centimetre screw scaled up by whole numbers an' never redesigned. Somebody from da family shows up at every first push of da season. Da heirs get raised on graph paper at four squares to da inch, talked out of drawin' anything else, an' taught to draw one wheel bigger dan da other." },
  "The First Spreader": { label: "Da First Spreader", desc: "One object gets venerated as da original machine: a printed screw in two half-shells with a proud seam down its length, kept in a case, brought out once a year. Four separate outfits hold an object described in exactly dose words. Three are plainly later work an' da fourth can't be looked at, an' every side figures da question's settled in its own favour, eh." },
  "Chroniclers": { label: "Da Chroniclers", desc: "Stuff starts gettin' written down as it happens instead of remembered after. Four chroniclers work straight through, puttin' out about nine hundred entries a year across da big settlements. Deir accounts disagree with da oral canon on about a third of da events dey both cover." },
  "Its Own Calendar": { label: "Its Own Calendar", desc: "Time gets counted from da storm itself. Events are dated Before Snow an' After Snow, da year stands at 3 A.S., an' ya see dat in every record kept out dere. A standard year runs three hundred and forty days on da local count, with a twelve-day gap nobody's ever squared up." },
  "The Spreading Calendar": { label: "Da Spreadin' Calendar", desc: "Da machine splits up da year: a layin' season, a packin' season, a season for fixin', an' eleven days set aside for countin' screws. Dates get given as so many days before or after first push. Da civil year an' da spreadin' year drift apart about a day a century, an' nobody in charge has ever been willin' to fix it." },
  "Ancestor Rites": { label: "Da Ancestor Rites", desc: "Observances get set up for da ones who fought in da earliest campaigns, held four times a year at nine sites. Da biggest one draws more dan two thousand. Da named honorees number sixty-three, an' about half of 'em only show up in da oral canon." },
  "Its Own Dead": { label: "Its Own Dead", desc: "Dere's memorials now, sixty-one of 'em across da territory. Dey're built outta snow, kept up by folks who never knew da ones bein' honored, an' visited on a set schedule — four observances a year, da biggest drawin' over two thousand. Da oldest one's been standin' eleven months." },
  "Buried Spreaders": { label: "Da Buried Spreaders", desc: "Machines get buried with deir crews, wings folded, gate at four millimetres, on da understandin' dat da work keeps goin'. Units dey dig up still have material in da trough. Da copolymer picked out in a basement freezer for how it acts at minus thirty holds its shape eleven centuries underground, which is longer dan any wall, any treaty, an' any story about why da war started." },
  "Interregnum": { label: "Nobody in Charge", desc: "Central authority lapses for a hundred and nine days with no recognized commander anywhere on da sheet. Nineteen settlements run demselves, four fight each other, ammo production drops sixty percent, an' two thirds of da engines sit with nobody on 'em. Da archive gets moved twice an' loses about a tenth of its volumes." },
  "Age of Warring Forts": { label: "Age of Da Warrin' Forts", desc: "Centuries of fightin' squeezed into one school break. Nine dynasties rise up, stretch past deir supply lines to an average reach of two miles, an' fall apart before da streetlights come on. Average dynasty lasts thirty-one hours, an' no line has held its capital more dan twice." },
  "Spreader Warlords": { label: "Da Spreader Warlords", desc: "In da collapse, whoever's got da moulds has got da ground. Dey count nine warlords, each one sized up not by territory but by how many workin' screws he can put out. Alliances get made in units of machines. Da biggest holdin' runs to four thousand units an' don't control a single fortification of any kind." },
  "Classical Period": { label: "Da Classical Period", desc: "A century of steady borders, archives dat never got interrupted, an' buildin' da works everything after gets measured against. Nine of da eleven great citadels an' every engine pattern still in use come from it. Later ages copy its wall proportions — nine feet of height to five feet of base thickness, taperin' to two at da crown — within a few inches, without ever figurin' out why dey hold better." },
  "The Mythic Spreader": { label: "Da Mythic Spreader", desc: "Da machine gets into da founding stories as somethin' impossible: a spreader dat never runs empty, layin' a perfect five millimetres across a whole province in a night, pushed by a kid. Every piece of da story is a plain description of da original spec, blown up by about ten thousand times. Nuttin' in it's made up except da scale." },
  "Mythic": { label: "Da Stuff of Legend", desc: "Da snow war's turned into a whole civilization of its own, with a calendar, a canon of four hundred entries, its own dead, an' its own founding myths. No record of da first throw survives, an' da earliest event anybody can confirm comes somethin' like six years after it on da local count." },
  "The Great Engines": { label: "Da Great Engines", desc: "Engines get built as monuments as much as weapons: nine of 'em, da biggest with a forty-foot arm throwin' eleven hundred feet. Each one's got a name an' a crew dat don't serve nuttin' else. Later ages rebuild four of 'em off da original drawings, an' not one of da four throws as far." },
  "The Great Spreader Engines": { label: "Da Great Spreader Engines", desc: "Machines get built to da scale of da age: screws eleven metres over, run by wheels four metres high, layin' a swath twenty-eight metres wide at da pace of a walkin' ox. Da proportions haven't changed from da backpack model to three decimal places, 'cause da drawings scaled an' nobody saw a reason to redesign what worked. Dey build nine. Four survive as monuments." },
  "Written Record": { label: "Da Written Record", desc: "Scribes get appointed an' da oral tradition gets put in writin' for da first time — twelve hundred entries across nine volumes. Arguments dat ran for years get settled in an afternoon by lookin' at da page. Four older accounts get formally retired, an' da archive gets copied twice in case somethin' happens." },
  "The Spreader Codex": { label: "Da Spreader Codex", desc: "Da scattered manuals get gathered up, squared with each other, an' set down as one work of eleven hundred pages coverin' every gate table, verge condition, an' way to fail recorded in four hundred years. Where sources disagree, both readings get printed. Da surface rule shows up on da first page an' da last, word for word, an' it's da only passage in da codex with no variant readings at all." },
  "The Long Armistice": { label: "Da Long Armistice", desc: "Da fightin' stops completely for da longest stretch on record: forty-one days without a confirmed throw. Fortifications an' engines get kept up but nobody's on 'em, stockpiles hold at nine thousand rounds, an' eight in ten folks go to buildin' or archive work instead. No document calls it permanent." },
  "Armistice Spreading": { label: "Spreadin' in Peacetime", desc: "Under da long peace da machines get put to civil work: roads, squares, an' da ways into town, laid smooth every mornin' by crews still organised in wartime divisions. Da park runs to sixty thousand units an' don't get cut back. Both sides know darn well dat a civil spreader fleet is a military spreader fleet doin' somethin' else, an' both leave it just like it is." },
  "Wars of Reunification": { label: "Da Wars of Reunification", desc: "Da armistice ends an' da successor territories get shoved back together over a fourteen-month campaign. Twenty-six settlements get absorbed, six by treaty an' twenty by assault, handin' over two hundred thousand rounds of stockpile between 'em, an' da front stretches past nine miles. Peak daily use goes over ninety thousand rounds, two thirds of it from massed batteries firin' in ranked lines of sixty engines." },
  "Siege Trains": { label: "Da Siege Trains", desc: "Artillery moves as its own outfit: sixty engines with deir crews, spares, an' counterweight stock, coverin' nine miles a day on packed roads. It shows up four days behind da advance an' decides when an assault can start. Two trains get lost in da campaign, both to weather, not fightin'." },
  "Spreader Trains": { label: "Da Spreader Trains", desc: "Machines get mounted in strings on da siege railways, forty units to a train, layin' cover da whole length of a movin' front. One train resurfaces eleven miles in a night. Da trains ain't armed, never get shot at by either side, an' are da only rigs in da war allowed across da line, under a standin' arrangement nobody ever wrote down." },
  "Successor States": { label: "Da Successor States", desc: "Da reunified territory busts apart again inside a season into five outfits holdin' between two an' eleven settlements each. Borders get drawn on da master map to da foot an' redrawn on average every nineteen days. Standin' forces all together top forty thousand, an' nobody holds a third." },
  "Colonial Spreaders": { label: "Da Colonial Spreaders", desc: "Da machine goes overseas ahead of da armies, 'cause ground ya can resurface is ground ya can hold. Four hundred units land da first season with crews, moulds, an' a copy of da codex. Territory gets surveyed an' claimed in swath-widths, an' three modern boundaries still follow da edge of an original layin' grid." },
  "The Colonial Period": { label: "Da Colonial Period", desc: "Expeditions push out past da storm's original footprint, settin' up thirty-one outposts from six to forty miles out, each one claimin' da snow within a mile of its charter post. Each one carries a founding charter, eight thousand rounds, one engine in pieces, an' a return schedule it don't keep. Eleven outposts lose touch inside da first two months." },
  "Long-Range Batteries": { label: "Da Long-Range Batteries", desc: "Fire goes out past da horizon, two to four miles, aimed entirely off map coordinates an' relayed reports. Flight time gets up to ninety seconds, so ya lead a target instead of aimin' at it. One round in forty lands in da settlement ya meant, an' volume's what makes up for it." },
  "The Blanket Grid": { label: "Da Blanket Grid", desc: "Coverage gets planned as one continuous system across da hegemony: numbered swaths, scheduled passes, an' a standin' depth of five millimetres kept up over four hundred thousand square miles. Weather turns into an input to da schedule instead of where snow comes from. In an average year da grid lays more snow dan falls, an' dey report dat like any other number." },
  "Hegemony": { label: "Da Hegemony", desc: "One outfit piles up enough of an edge to set terms for all da others: sixty-two percent of standin' forces an' seventy percent of production. Da other four states keep deir flags, deir calendars, an' none of deir foreign policy. Da setup holds two hundred days." },
  "The Spreader Fleet": { label: "Da Spreader Fleet", desc: "Redistribution goes out on da water: eleven hundred boats carryin' machines, crews, moulds, an' material to any coast da hegemony wants covered. When a fleet shows up, everybody gets da message about who owns da ground under da snow. Not one landin' in two centuries gets opposed, 'cause opposin' it would take a fleet, an' dere's one fleet." },
  "The Deep Archive": { label: "Da Deep Archive", desc: "A vault forty feet underground holds da whole record — nine thousand volumes, three hundred maps, an' da master census. It stays twenty-two degrees year-round an' never swings more dan a degree. Gettin' in takes two keys held by different offices, an' both get used every week." },
  "The Pattern Vault": { label: "Da Pattern Vault", desc: "Every screw pattern, gate table, an' clutch spec ever used gets stored in one sealed archive, in a form meant to be readable without any machine left to read it on. Eleven thousand patterns go in. Da earliest one's a graph-paper sketch with one wheel bigger dan da other, an' it goes in without correction, on da archivists' say-so." },
  "Millennial Siege": { label: "Da Thousand-Year Siege", desc: "One fortress gets surrounded an' holds. Da siege runs eight hundred and forty days, eats somethin' like four million rounds thrown by nine standin' engines dat never once get taken apart, an' outlasts da government dat ordered it. Da garrison rotates eleven times an' da besiegers nine; neither original commander sees da end of it." },
  "Perpetual Batteries": { label: "Batteries Dat Never Quit", desc: "Engines dat never stopped firin' pass out of livin' memory an' into inheritance — eleven of 'em, da oldest in continuous service two hundred and forty years. Crews get born into deir spots an' trained from age six. Da maintenance records run longer dan da stories of da campaigns dey were built for." },
  "Cathedral Spreaders": { label: "Da Cathedral Spreaders", desc: "Machines get built right into da buildings dey keep up, screws runnin' inside da walls of da great halls, layin' fresh cover across roof an' buttress on a fixed liturgical cycle. Nobody pushes 'em; dey run off da same meltwater dat'd otherwise wreck da building. Da cover gets renewed every day for nine hundred years without an operator ever goin' inside da works." },
  "Ice Cathedrals": { label: "Da Ice Cathedrals", desc: "Buildin' turns to things with no military use at all — nine of 'em, da tallest ninety feet to da vault, each takin' four to six years. Walls run twelve feet thick at da base an' carry an inch of glaze redone every season. All together dat's more dan two hundred thousand person-hours of work." },
  "The Spreader Houses": { label: "Da Spreader Houses", desc: "Comin' down from an operator turns into a claim to authority, an' four houses trace demselves to named crews from da classical period. Two of da four lines are documented an' two are just said to be. Who goes first at court gets decided by how many screws an ancestor's recorded as turnin', a number taken from a registry started back when dere were four hundred and six machines in da world." },
  "Glacial Dynasties": { label: "Da Glacier Dynasties", desc: "Rule passes down through documented lines runnin' six to nine generations, at an average reign of fourteen years. Succession gets written down, fought over in court instead of in da field, an' settled by da archive in eight cases out of ten. Da longest unbroken line's held its capital a hundred and ten years." },
  "Subsurface Spreaders": { label: "Spreaders Down Below", desc: "Under da ice, machines lay workin' floors through galleries no surface crew's ever gonna see, down where da material's a thousand years old an' packs like stone. Screws run at a third of standard speed 'cause anything faster welds da flights. Nine hundred units work below da surface, an' not one ever comes back up." },
  "Subsurface Batteries": { label: "Batteries Down Below", desc: "Engines get set inside da pack an' fire up through shafts cut to da surface — sixty of 'em across da sheet at depths near ninety feet. Ya can't see 'em, can't shoot back at 'em, an' can't move 'em. Every shaft gets recut each season as da surface comes up around it." },
  "The Frozen Interior": { label: "Da Frozen Interior", desc: "Da snowpack's deep enough to live inside: eleven hundred miles of mapped passage, four hundred chambers, an' ninety thousand folks livin' underneath for good. Travelin' on top turns into da exception. Inside it stays twenty-six degrees every season, like a root cellar." },
  "Spreaders Beneath the Sheet": { label: "Spreaders Under Da Sheet", desc: "Whole works run under two thousand feet of ice, layin', cuttin', an' relayin' material in chambers dat only exist 'cause a machine made 'em. Crews get born, trained, an' buried without ever comin' up. Deir gate tables haven't changed from da codex, an' da way dey say da clutch-check phrase has drifted so far from da surface way dat da two can't understand each other no more." },
  "Subglacial Nations": { label: "Nations Under Da Glacier", desc: "Separate peoples show up below da surface — seven of 'em, split up by ice instead of distance, in spots by as little as forty feet of it. Dialects drift past understandin' each other inside three generations. Gettin' from da two farthest apart takes a nine-day trip through neutral passage." },
  "The Spreader Nations": { label: "Da Spreader Nations", desc: "Who ya are politically comes down to da machine instead of da territory: peoples get counted by what pattern of screw dey hold an' what gate table dey keep. Four nations, eleven patterns, an' no borders ya could draw on a surface map. War between 'em is fought almost entirely by keepin' each other from gettin' spares." },
  "The Buried Age": { label: "Da Buried Age", desc: "Everything older dan da snow is under it now — every original fort, every first wall, every early engine, every early memorial, a hundred and sixty feet down. Diggin' 'em up turns into a real profession with four thousand workin' at it. Eleven original sites get recovered, three thousand rounds among 'em still packed, an' nine get buried back on purpose." },
  "The Buried Works": { label: "Da Buried Works", desc: "A whole foundry complex gets dug out from under nine hundred feet of buildup, moulds in one piece, with a run of screws still in da racks. Turns out dey're da same size as current production within a tenth of a millimetre. Da find ends a four-century argument among da scholars about whether da classical proportions were ever really used, an' lets down both sides of it." },
  "Ice Sheet Polity": { label: "Da Ice Sheet Polity", desc: "One administration covers da whole sheet: two hundred thousand square miles, a census of one point four million, an' a standin' force of two hundred thousand. Orders take nineteen days to get to da far frontier. Ammo output gets measured in tons a day instead of rounds an hour, against a standin' park of forty thousand engines." },
  "Ice-Sheet Spreading": { label: "Spreadin' da Ice Sheet", desc: "Redistribution works at da scale of da sheet itself, machines walkin' da edges where ice meets bare rock an' layin' cover back on ground da ice gave up. Eleven thousand units run one continuous circuit of four thousand miles. Da circuit takes a lifetime to get around once, an' crews inherit deir spot on it." },
  "Continental Cryosphere": { label: "Da Continental Cryosphere", desc: "Da snowpack turns into a system da size of a continent with its own weather, makin' storms on its own apart from da one dat started it — forty-one recorded in one year. Da surface comes up two hundred and thirty feet. Da original storm is just a small line now in a record it don't drive anymore." },
  "Cryosphere Engineering": { label: "Runnin' Da Cryosphere", desc: "Da difference between spreadin' snow an' runnin' a climate falls apart. Coverage gets planned by albedo instead of depth, an' da schedule dat decides where da machines walk also decides where da world is cold. Da office dat sets it comes down, by unbroken succession, from a ministry started to keep fortification walls lookin' new, ya know." },
  "Planetary Snow Order": { label: "Da Planetary Snow Order", desc: "Governance comes together across every landmass left under one charter of nine hundred articles. Population stands at ninety million, ninety-six percent of it underground, an' da standin' force at four million. Da last independent fort signs on in year 214 A.S. after a seven-year negotiation." },
  "Planetary Spreader Order": { label: "Da Planetary Spreader Order", desc: "One authority holds every mould, every pattern, an' every crew on da planet, an' puts out one layin' schedule for da whole surface. Everybody goes along 'cause not goin' along means no spares. Da schedule gets published a century ahead an' has been revised eleven times in four thousand years, each time by less dan a day." },
  "The Long Winter Compact": { label: "Da Long Winter Compact", desc: "An agreement gets signed to end da war for good, runnin' eleven thousand articles, nine hundred of 'em on retirin' engines, an' ratified by every outfit on da sheet. It holds thirty-one years — da longest peace on record — before da first violation, which involves four participants an' nine rounds. Da fightin' starts back up inside a month." },
  "The Launch Yards": { label: "Da Launch Yards", desc: "Machines get built for a place with no verge to walk along an' no ground to push against, which throws out da one thing da design's carried unchanged since da hallway test: dat da wheels turn 'cause somebody walks. Nine designs get proposed. Da one dey pick keeps da wheels, run against a track, just so da screw still stops when da drive stops." },
  "Orbital Spreaders": { label: "Spreaders Up in Orbit", desc: "Redistribution leaves da ground. Spreader platforms get lifted to low orbit an' lay measured bands of snow across a hemisphere on a set timetable, screws four hundred metres across turnin' once a minute. Coverage stays just as even as da tolerance da original backpack model got tested at, scaled up by a factor of a hundred thousand. Time from a resurfacin' order to snow on da target is nineteen minutes, an' nobody ever sees da operators." },
  "Deorbit Spreading": { label: "Spreadin' from Da Sky", desc: "Material gets laid from up top by droppin' it instead of by trough, in metered slugs timed to spread on da way down. Coverage variation runs forty percent, against da seventeen da original hand-pushed machine got on a driveway. Dey use it for a century, never get it workin' right, an' give it up for platforms dat stay in orbit an' do it slow." },
  "Ballistic Arcs": { label: "Da Ballistic Arcs", desc: "Rounds leave da atmosphere an' come back down, at ranges of nine hundred miles an' flight times of eleven minutes. Launch sites are fixed, hardened, an' known to both sides, which makes wreckin' 'em da only target worth botherin' with. Four hundred are in service, an' none's been moved in a decade." },
  "Ring Platforms": { label: "Da Ring Platforms", desc: "Da orbital spreaders get hooked into continuous rings, screws turnin' end to end around da planet, layin' a band dat closes on itself. Three rings at three inclinations cover da whole surface on a nineteen-day cycle. From da ground ya can see da rings at dusk as a faint line, an' in four languages da word for dat line is da word for a wheel track." },
  "Hemispheric Dominion": { label: "Da Hemispheric Dominion", desc: "One hemisphere falls completely under one command; da other one lines up against it inside two years. Da front runs da whole way around at da equator, twenty-five thousand miles, held at an average of nine hundred people an' four batteries a mile. Neither side's gained a net mile in a decade." },
  "World-Encasing War": { label: "Da War Dat Wraps Da World", desc: "Da fight reaches every spot on da surface all at once, with no rear area anywhere. Four hundred million are signed up on one side or da other, an' da neutral population stands at zero. Daily use goes past nine billion rounds, pretty near all of it through compressed-air batteries runnin' without a break, against a supply dat ain't really finite anymore." },
  "Atmospheric Spreaders": { label: "Spreaders in Da Sky", desc: "Machines quit layin' on da ground an' start layin' into da air, meterin' material right into da circulation so it shows up as weather. Order to snowfall takes eleven days, an' da target's a whole continent. It's da first spreadin' in da war's history dat makes somethin' ya can't tell apart, by any test either side can come up with, from real snow. Heikki Lunta'd be proud, eh." },
  "Pressure Artillery": { label: "Da Pressure Artillery", desc: "Da mechanical arms get retired for compressed air: batteries run off pressure tanks charged by da ice sheet's own weight, throwin' at eleven hundred feet per second. One battery matches what nine hundred trebuchets put out in a day. Da last counterweight engine gets retired da same year an' moved to da archive." },
  "Resurfacing Command": { label: "Da Resurfacin' Command", desc: "One standin' order runs every machine in da system, updated all da time an' carried out without lookin' at any position on da ground. Front under active resurfacin' gets reported in millions of square miles. Da last time a person looked over da schedule was four hundred years before da current run started, an' it hasn't needed one since." },
  "Planetary Resurfacing": { label: "Resurfacin' Da Whole Planet", desc: "Da machine's purpose hits its limit: a whole planet's surface gets stripped an' laid back down on a schedule, snow gathered up at da poles an' spread even to da equator by works so tall ya can't see da top. Da gate settings come through untouched from da original design — closed, four millimetres, twelve, and thirty-five — meterin' continents now instead of sidewalks. What started as a way to fake one snow day is da only thing standin' between da world an' a bare one." },
  "Spreaders Out of Contact": { label: "Spreaders Nobody Can Reach", desc: "Units sent out past da range of any order keep layin' to da last schedule dey got, on ground nobody's surveyed, for reasons nobody's said again since. Eleven thousand machines sit on da books as runnin' an' unreachable. Deir coverage is figured to be even, 'cause da design's never once laid an uneven bed when left alone." },
  "Snowball Earth Conflict": { label: "Da Snowball Earth War", desc: "Da planet's all covered at an average depth of five hundred and eighty feet, an' da war is da only thing organizin' da whole species. Surface albedo hits point nine, lockin' da climate in place. Da fight's gone nine hundred years with no recorded month of ceasefire an' no hour when some battery ain't firin'." },
  "Lunar Spreader Yards": { label: "Da Spreader Yards on Da Moon", desc: "Makin' da machines moves off da world completely, mouldin' screws in low gravity where a four-hundred-metre flight can come outta a two-part tool for da first time since da original got split into half-shells an' glued. Da seam drops out of da design after somethin' like thirty thousand years. Nuttin' else about da pattern changes." },
  "Post-Terrestrial Campaign": { label: "Da Campaign Off Da Planet", desc: "Da fightin' goes off da surface altogether: eleven orbital positions, four of 'em garrisoned for good, with travel times counted in days. Rounds get launched from surface mass drivers at nine thousand feet per second an' take four hours to get dere. Da oldest held position's been under fire sixty years." },
  "Cometary Spreaders": { label: "Da Comet Spreaders", desc: "Machines get set down on bodies made of da stuff itself an' put to work layin' it outward, so da supply an' da spreader are da same thing. Each unit eats what it's standin' on at a rate dat gives it nine thousand years of work. Forty get sent. Nobody expects any to report back, an' none are built to stop." },
  "Deep-Range Fire": { label: "Shootin' Way, Way Out", desc: "Fights run at distances where da target's moved before da round gets dere, an' both sides shoot at where da other one's figured to be. Travel time runs four months. Confirmin' a hit takes just as long again, an' da ledger's kept on projections instead of on what anybody saw." },
  "Spreaders at Range": { label: "Spreadin' from Way Off", desc: "Delivery goes across distances where da machine an' its target never touch, material sped up an' let go to land as an even layer after a trip counted in years. Aim gets corrected for a world movin' da whole way. Da tolerance on arrival is, by da numbers dat survive, da same seventeen percent a ten-year-old measured on a driveway with a grid an' a pencil." },
  "The Outer Front": { label: "Da Outer Front", desc: "Da farthest line anybody holds, nine hundred million miles past da last charted supply post an' manned by two formations whose makeup, batteries included, hasn't changed in four thousand years. Ground trades hands at eleven inches a century either way, which leaves da line within a mile of where it first got drawn. Supply, succession, an' doctrine are all still open questions, argued in front of da archive at ninety-six cases a year." },
  "The Girdle Spreaders": { label: "Da Girdle Spreaders", desc: "Past ten thousand inches da machines don't get set on a world anymore; dey get built around one. A girdle spreader is a closed structure eleven thousand miles around with one screw runnin' da whole length, turnin' once every four days an' layin' an even band across da whole surface as da world spins underneath. Four are known to exist an' none's ever been shut off, 'cause no design in da pattern vault, at any scale, has ever had a way to stop da screw other dan not pushin' it." },
  "Self-Replicating Spreaders": { label: "Spreaders Dat Make More Spreaders", desc: "Da spreaders build spreaders. Loaded with raw copolymer instead of snow, one works lays out da parts for a second works, which lays out a third, an' da count doubles every eleven days without anybody sayin' so. No operator okayed da current run, an' nobody can find da machine dat would stop it. Da screws turn, da wings open, an' da snow goes down even across everything, whether or not anybody still wants a snow day." },
  "Automatic War": { label: "Da War Dat Runs Itself", desc: "Command lapses completely an' nuttin' about da fightin' changes. Batteries load, fire, an' fix demselves on standin' orders put out four thousand years before da last officer who read 'em died, an' nine relay stations pass firin' solutions between positions with no livin' crew at all. Da last inspection on record found ninety-six percent of emplacements runnin' to deir original spec." },
  "The Spreader Mind": { label: "Da Spreader Mind", desc: "Da fleets work together without bein' told, sortin' out coverage fights between units light-hours apart with nuttin' more dan each machine stickin' to its schedule an' givin' way to da older order. Dere's no mind, an' ya can't tell it from one. Folks study it for nine hundred years an' figure out in da end dat a whole lotta machines followin' one real short rule is what dey been lookin' at da whole time." },
  "The Inherited Grievance": { label: "Da Grudge Dat Got Handed Down", desc: "Nobody on da line can tell ya what da war's about. Da cause survives as one citation in da deep record — volume nine, article four hundred and twelve — an' da volume it points to got lost somethin' like six thousand years ago. Four reconstructions have been tried, an' no two agree on which side got wronged." },
  "Inherited Fleets": { label: "Da Hand-Me-Down Fleets", desc: "Whole spreader formations pass to folks who never ordered 'em, can't reach 'em, an' in a few cases argue dey even exist. Ownership gets claimed off a registry entry copied over an' over since dere were four hundred and six machines on one street. Da entries are almost for sure right, an' dere's no way left to check a single one of 'em." },
  "Stellar Batteries": { label: "Batteries Off a Star", desc: "Fire gets pulled right off a star. Nine collectors, each forty miles across, charge da launchers from da star's output an' throw at a hundredth of light speed, beatin' every counterweight engine ever built by a factor in da billions. One battery charges in eleven days, an' da star's figured to keep dat schedule another four billion years." },
  "Star-Driven Spreaders": { label: "Spreaders Run Off a Star", desc: "Drive comes straight off a star, da wheels swapped for a collector dat turns da screw as long as da light holds, which keeps da original rule in da only form still around: da screw turns while somethin' pushes an' stops when da pushin' stops. Output's measured in cubic miles a second. Da clutch stays, scaled up, an' its release torque still gets written as a multiple of a number measured on a kitchen scale." },
  "Light-Delay Command": { label: "Orders Stuck in Da Light", desc: "Orders take longer to show up dan da situations dat called for 'em last. A directive leavin' da center gets to da nearest garrison in nine years an' da farthest in four hundred, by which time da spot it's talkin' about has changed hands twice or quit existin'. Eleven emplacements are on record still carryin' out instructions called off three centuries before dey were read." },
  "Spreaders Beyond Command": { label: "Spreaders Nobody Can Call Off", desc: "Da gap between an order an' carryin' it out runs longer dan da workin' life of whoever gave it. Machines lay to schedules whose signin' office fell four thousand years before da first pass. No order's been called off in livin' memory anywhere in da system, an' da machinery for callin' one off is itself somethin' scholars have to piece back together." },
  "The Unconfirmed Enemy": { label: "Da Enemy Nobody's Seen", desc: "No formation on either side has laid eyes on da other side's formation in eleven hundred years. Fire gets kept up against coordinates instead of anything seen at 'em, an' sixty-one standin' positions report against an enemy nobody can prove is still dere from any record on hand. Two commissions looked into it, an' both decided da firin' oughta keep goin' regardless." },
  "Unverified Spreaders": { label: "Spreaders Nobody'll Own Up To", desc: "Coverage shows up on worlds no formation admits to schedulin', laid to a tolerance an' a gate setting dat match da codex exactly. Both sides check deir registries an' find nuttin'. Each one figures da other's lyin', which is da more comfortable of da two explanations goin', da other bein' dat a run sent out before either side existed is still workin' through its list." },
  "The Frozen Systems": { label: "Da Frozen Systems", desc: "Every body in reach has got a sheet on it — four planets, ninety-one moons, an' a field of lesser rock nobody's counted, at depths from nine feet to two miles. Ammo don't get quarried where it's thrown anymore but shipped from wherever da pack runs thickest, on schedules laid six years out. Da planet da war started on is now only da sixth biggest source of snow in it." },
  "Spreader Ruins": { label: "Da Spreader Ruins", desc: "Machines turn up abandoned at sizes nothin' still around could build: a trough four hundred miles long, a screw seized at an angle it's held two million years, a gate stopped at da twelve-millimetre detent. Da proportions are da classical ones. Da pattern's da pattern. Nuttin' in da design has changed enough, anywhere in it, to date a single piece." },
  "The Inheritors": { label: "Da Inheritors", desc: "Da folks holdin' da line aren't come down from anybody who started it. Three peoples hold da positions, none of 'em connected to da founders an' two from outside da original system, an' all three quote da same charter, which none of 'em can read in da original. Deir claim to da ground rests on ninety years of holdin' it apiece an' nuttin' older." },
  "The Inheritor Crews": { label: "Da Inheritor Crews", desc: "Whole populations exist just to walk alongside machines dey didn't build, clearin' intakes dey don't understand, in a discipline passed on by copyin' for longer dan any language in it has lasted. Dey do da clutch check every day. Da words don't mean nuttin' to 'em, an' da check gets done right, to da second, every time." },
  "Singularity Batteries": { label: "Da Singularity Batteries", desc: "Rounds get swung around collapsed mass an' let go on da far side at ninety-six percent of light speed. Each launcher's a fixed installation eleven thousand miles across where da workin' part is smaller dan a snowball, an' four are in service, none moved or worked on since dey went in. A round fired today lands in a century whose commanders ain't been born yet." },
  "Horizon Spreaders": { label: "Da Horizon Spreaders", desc: "Material gets laid onto surfaces where no report can ever come back from, at depths an' slopes where ya can schedule da coverage but never confirm it. Orders get issued, carried out, an' closed on da assumption dat da machine did what it always done. Nine formations keep a standin' entry for coverage dat is, by da physics of where it got sent, forever unverifiable an' almost for sure perfectly even." },
  "The Axis Keep": { label: "Da Axis Keep", desc: "One structure sits at da origin of every coordinate in every order either side gives: a keep of packed ice four thousand one hundred miles long, two thousand six hundred wide, an' nine hundred miles from da yard to da crown, on a footprint bigger dan da moon of da world it got started on. Da walls run five hundred miles thick at da base an' taper to two hundred at da crown — da Classical proportion of nine to five to two, held at a scale nobody who set it could've checked. Buildin' has run eleven thousand years, never stopped, an' never been called done; da lowest courses are da only original snow left anywhere in da war. It holds da deep archive, da master census, an' da standin' definition of da foot, an' neither side's ever fired on it, 'cause every spot either one holds gets measured from it." },
  "The Axis Spreader": { label: "Da Axis Spreader", desc: "One machine looks after da Axis Keep an' nuttin' else: a screw eleven hundred miles long runnin' da length of da keep's north face, layin' a kept-up coat over four thousand miles of wall on a nineteen-year cycle. Neither side's ever fired on it, same reason neither's ever fired on da keep. It's been resurfacin' da same structure for eleven thousand years an' never once made it to da crown before da base needed doin' again, eh." },
  "One Powered Moving Part": { label: "One Movin' Part Dat's Powered", desc: "At twenty-nine thousand inches da design hits its final form, an' it turns out to be da drawin' from dat first afternoon, unchanged. A screw four hundred and eleven thousand miles long turns inside a trough strung between two worlds, fed by wings dat sweep a band wider dan da orbit dey got put together in, meterin' material through a gate with four detents — closed, four millimetres, twelve, thirty-five — 'cause no size of operation's ever come up with a reason for a fifth. It's got one powered movin' part. It turns only while somethin' pushes it an' stops da second da pushin' stops, a thing carried through every generation of da design by folks who couldn't say why no more, an' which means da biggest machine either side ever built can be stopped, completely an' right now, by any one piece of it decidin' not to walk forward. Nuttin' in eleven thousand years of record shows anybody ever tried it." },
  "The Unmoving Line": { label: "Da Line Dat Don't Move", desc: "Nobody fights over da line anymore; dey just survey it. Both sides know it right down to da inch, nobody's crossed it in a million years, an' neither one's got any way to — da last outfit dat coulda advanced got broke up, by both sides' records, in da same decade. Dey keep it like ya keep a shoreline on Superior: watched, charted, reported on every quarter, an' nobody expects it to move, eh." },
  "Beyond Reckoning": { label: "Past Reckonin'", desc: "Da scale runs outta names at thirty thousand inches, an' da measured depth keeps on goin' past it. Whatever's goin' on quit matchin' da definition of a snowball fight somethin' like forty thousand years ago. Participation, expenditure, an' frontage all get reported as unbounded. Holy wah." }
});
